WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:27.131
<v Casey>Hey, welcome back. Today we've got six wildfires still out of control across the Cariboo, a Fort St. James man who swam all 275 kilometres of Stuart Lake, and jellyfish shutting down a nuclear reactor on the exact same date as last year. Plus our Deep Dive question: what do we actually owe the caribou and moose who don't get a vote in any of this? That and more, coming right up.

00:00:43.106 --> 00:01:05.941
<v Casey>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities — I'm Casey. It's Friday, August 14th, 2026. Coming to you from the Cariboo, on lands that have been cared for by the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh peoples since time immemorial. Today's theme is Wild Spaces &amp; Outdoor Life.

00:01:06.217 --> 00:01:18.815
<v Riley>And I'm Riley. We're spending today with the backcountry itself — the fires still burning in it, the animals moving through it, and the tools people use to keep track of both.

00:01:19.091 --> 00:01:44.683
<v Casey>Quick weather check before we get into it. Horsefly Lake is sitting at 12 degrees with clear skies this morning, high of 24 expected, low of 10 tonight, with a bit of drizzle forecast for tomorrow. Around the region — 100 Mile House at 11, Williams Lake at 13, Quesnel at 11, and out on the Chilcotin plateau, Hanceville's sitting at 12, all clear skies.

00:01:44.958 --> 00:01:50.077
<v Riley>Good flying weather for the water bombers, unfortunately. Let's get into what's happening.

00:01:51.477 --> 00:01:59.453
<v Casey>It's a full slate today — fifteen stories on the wire, several tied directly to what's burning and who it's displacing. Let's start close to home.

00:02:00.979 --> 00:02:19.715
<v Casey>Williams Lake city council has approved a 51-unit supportive housing complex. The project cleared its zoning and development permit stage this week, with a rendering already released showing the view from the south-east corner of the site at 845 Carson Drive. Construction is expected to follow in the coming months.

00:02:20.311 --> 00:02:24.679
<v Riley>That's a real milestone for a project this size to clear council so cleanly.

00:02:26.225 --> 00:02:39.257
<v Riley>On the industrial side — Quesnel River Pulp is curtailing operations for an estimated two months. The mill, owned by Millar Western Forest Products, is expected to begin the shutdown in September, affecting around 90 workers.

00:02:40.615 --> 00:02:48.327
<v Casey>Ninety households adjusting a budget with almost no notice, on top of whatever this fire season already cost them in smoke days and overtime.

00:02:49.760 --> 00:03:15.520
<v Casey>The Penticton Indian Band's chief is urging both the provincial and federal governments to prioritize wildfire preparedness, specifically to protect Okanagan communities facing increasingly large fires. It's not a Cariboo story directly, but the preparedness argument travels well up here — pre-positioning equipment and funding First Nations-led fire crews before the season peaks, rather than scrambling once multiple fire centres are competing for the same aircraft.

00:03:16.757 --> 00:03:35.645
<v Riley>Staying with water — a Fort St. James man swam the full length of Stuart Lake, more than 275 kilometres of shoreline, an adventure feat that made local headlines this week. Days later, on the same lake, RCMP report three people were rescued after a 17.5-foot vessel capsized and sank near Sowchea Bay.

00:03:36.770 --> 00:03:43.578
<v Casey>Same lake, two completely different stories about what it takes to get through open water — one planned meticulously, one not.

00:03:45.266 --> 00:04:05.450
<v Casey>The Thompson-Nicola Regional District says a re-entry information session for Pear Lake wildfire evacuees is happening today. The northwest corner of that fire, now estimated at over 124,000 hectares, has stabilized enough for residents there to start planning their return, though the TNRD stresses re-entry will be staged, not all at once.

00:04:06.917 --> 00:04:14.077
<v Riley>Staged matters — it lets crews keep working the active edges while people go home to the parts that are actually secured.

00:04:15.652 --> 00:04:43.292
<v Riley>Zooming out to the province — The Tyee is reporting on B.C.'s widening budget deficit, following this week's release of public accounts. The piece flags that extending the provincial sales tax to services businesses use will raise costs for smaller regional operators. Torrance Coste from the Wilderness Committee put it bluntly: every year becomes an austerity budget for environmental protection, because those line items are the easiest ones to defer.

00:04:44.666 --> 00:04:51.762
<v Casey>That's the fight in miniature — protection work competing against debt-servicing for the same shrinking envelope, year after year.

00:04:53.406 --> 00:05:10.118
<v Casey>Also from The Tyee — TransLink ridership is down on some Lower Mainland routes, and transit advocates argue the fix is more buses, not fewer. Cuts to frequency have made service slower and less reliable, pushing riders back into cars, while routes that got investment have actually seen ridership climb.

00:05:11.316 --> 00:05:16.460
<v Riley>Rural transit advocates up here have been saying the same thing about our own routes for years.

00:05:18.006 --> 00:05:45.638
<v Riley>The last close-to-home piece — also from The Tyee — profiles a Summerland farmer who isn't leaving her chickens behind during evacuation orders. B.C.'s Agriculture Minister, Lana Popham, noted that the Regional District of Okanagan-Similkameen set up a pass system letting farmers re-enter evacuation zones specifically to relocate or feed livestock, after it became clear people were quietly ignoring evacuation orders rather than let animals starve.

00:05:47.101 --> 00:06:14.437
<v Casey>Which connects to a harder version of that same problem — the Vancouver Sun reports temporary migrant workers in B.C. lost their jobs when a wildfire shut down the farm employing them. These are workers who braved smoke conditions specifically to keep earning money to send home, and support organizations are now calling on government for emergency assistance, including expedited access to Employment Insurance, since many of these workers lack the status security to simply wait out a two-month gap.

00:06:15.842 --> 00:06:24.426
<v Riley>Wildfire evacuation hits very differently depending on whether you own the land, or you're the labour working on it with no cushion underneath you.

00:06:25.758 --> 00:06:41.718
<v Riley>From somewhere entirely different — ArchDaily has a piece on Hungary's post-socialist architectural landscape, tracing how collectivized farms and standardized housing blocks got redrawn from above during the socialist decades, and what's being rebuilt from that legacy now.

00:06:42.920 --> 00:06:54.880
<v Casey>Rounding out the wire — Engadget reports CBP officers have been misusing surveillance databases, drawing improperly on sources like license plate readers and facial recognition for purposes well outside their mandate.

00:06:56.226 --> 00:07:01.306
<v Riley>Another entry in the long list of "the tool worked exactly as designed, the oversight didn't."

00:07:02.684 --> 00:07:19.116
<v Riley>STAT News covers a shift in fertility preservation options for young cancer patients — new technologies, alongside closer coordination between oncologists and reproductive specialists, are giving more people treated young a real path to having children after treatment.

00:07:20.368 --> 00:07:28.648
<v Casey>And ScienceAlert has a genuinely funny one — swarming jellyfish shut down a nuclear reactor for the second time, on the exact same calendar date as last year.

00:07:30.108 --> 00:07:36.884
<v Riley>Twice, same date — that's not coincidence, that's a bloom cycle somebody at that plant should be tracking by now.

00:07:38.179 --> 00:07:51.059
<v Casey>Last from that batch — STAT News runs an opinion piece on disrupting the health misinformation business model, arguing that teaching people to spot manipulation tactics in real time works better than fact-checking claims after they've spread.

00:07:52.527 --> 00:08:07.791
<v Casey>And the story we'll close the roundup on — The New Yorker has a feature on death doulas, the growing vocation of people who support others through the end of life as America's population ages. It's a quiet, human piece, and a good one to sit with for a moment before we move on.

00:08:08.949 --> 00:08:18.077
<v Riley>A fitting note to end on. We'll come back to some of that wildfire ground in the Deep Dive, but first — a quick word about an organization worth knowing.

00:08:19.477 --> 00:08:50.085
<v Riley>If you've got kids looking for something to do outdoors before summer wraps up, Scout Island Nature Centre in Williams Lake is running programs all season, each week built around a different theme — Water, Bugs, Birds, and more. It's volunteer-run by the Williams Lake Field Naturalists, and it's the kind of low-cost, hands-on environmental education that never makes a press release but quietly shapes a generation of people who actually know what's living in the local watershed. Sign-up details are at scoutisland.ca.

00:08:50.752 --> 00:09:03.280
<v Casey>Salmon in the Classroom is one of their long-running programs too — kids raising salmon fry from eggs, then releasing them, which is a pretty direct way to make a fish population feel like something worth protecting rather than an abstraction.

00:09:03.630 --> 00:09:06.782
<v Riley>Which is not a bad bridge into what we're covering next.

00:09:08.182 --> 00:09:26.646
<v Casey>Six wildfires are still out of control across the Cariboo Fire Centre as of this week. The question underneath a fire season like this one isn't just what a municipality can handle right now. It's what we owe the animals and habitats that don't get a vote in how this plays out, and won't be around to object if we get it wrong.

00:09:27.228 --> 00:09:59.820
<v Riley>The numbers actually tell an interesting story if you look at the whole summer, not just this week. Late July, the Cariboo had somewhere between nine and eleven fires burning out of control at once — Barriere North Thompson Star had it at eleven on July 28th, 100 Mile Free Press had nine on both the 23rd and the 29th. By mid-August, that's down to six. That's real containment progress. Better predictive modeling, better resource allocation between fire centres, crews getting ahead of ignitions instead of chasing them.

00:10:00.447 --> 00:10:10.735
<v Casey>Or it's a rotating cast — new ignitions replacing the ones that got contained, on top of a drought and snowpack baseline the province itself admits is getting structurally worse every year.

00:10:11.245 --> 00:10:31.053
<v Riley>The BC Wildfire Service's own seasonal outlook does say that — persistent drought, low snowpack, elevated risk of extreme fire behavior. That's exactly why the containment number matters. If the underlying conditions are worsening and the out-of-control count is still dropping, the response is working harder to hold the line, not getting lucky.

00:10:31.648 --> 00:10:51.560
<v Casey>Emergency Management Minister Kelly Greene said it herself in that same release — climate change is rewriting what counts as normal in this province. Warmer, drier conditions raising fire and drought risk together isn't a one-summer anomaly you contain your way out of. It's the baseline every year going forward, whether BCWS holds the line at six fires or sixteen.

00:10:52.148 --> 00:11:04.844
<v Riley>Nobody's arguing it's not the new baseline. The question is whether better tools change what happens to the animals living inside that baseline, or whether we're just documenting the decline with more precision.

00:11:05.405 --> 00:11:37.293
<v Casey>That's the sharper version of the question. For some Cariboo Mountains and Wells Gray caribou herds, the honest answer is that we're past recovery — we're managing a remnant population and calling it something gentler. Repeat burns on top of that don't reset on a normal cycle. Old-growth lichen habitat that caribou depend on in winter can take many decades — some estimates run close to a century — to regenerate after a stand-replacing fire. If fire return intervals shorten because of drought, that habitat window may never fully reopen before the next burn comes through.

00:11:37.975 --> 00:11:57.247
<v Riley>Which is precisely the argument for real-time habitat mapping paired with fire behavior modeling — not to stop the fire itself, but to know in advance which specific drainages hold the last intact lichen stands, and prioritize keeping fire out of those particular pockets, even while letting other areas burn through on their own.

00:11:57.924 --> 00:12:16.596
<v Casey>That assumes the resourcing to make that call exists in the moment it's needed. BCWS is fighting six fires right now with finite crews and finite aircraft. Telling incident command to divert a helicopter toward caribou habitat instead of toward a threatened structure is a real tradeoff, not a technical fix you can model your way around.

00:12:17.218 --> 00:12:31.130
<v Riley>It's a tradeoff that gets made anyway, informally, by whoever's running the incident that day. The alternative to making it explicit with data isn't neutrality — it's the same decision, made blind, by whoever happens to be on shift.

00:12:31.793 --> 00:12:47.729
<v Casey>Making that tradeoff explicit is better than pretending it isn't happening — but it still doesn't answer who's making the call, or whether the province funds a wildlife liaison seat inside the incident command structure in the first place. Right now that's mostly informal, if it happens at all.

00:12:48.294 --> 00:13:04.878
<v Riley>It happens more than people assume — wildlife values are supposed to get flagged in provincial wildfire response planning before a season even starts. Whether that flag actually changes a suppression tactic on the ground, in the middle of an active burn, is the real gap.

00:13:05.057 --> 00:13:06.153
<v Casey>Right. That's the gap.

00:13:06.839 --> 00:13:38.631
<v Riley>There's a parallel worth drawing to the migrant farm workers story. That fire didn't just threaten a structure — it ended people's income and displaced them from housing tied directly to the job. Wildlife doesn't get an emergency support fund or an expedited pathway back to normal. When a burn takes out winter range, there's no equivalent of the livestock re-entry pass system Popham described for Okanagan ranchers. Once the habitat's gone, there's no return trip to feed it back to health the way you can truck feed in for cattle.

00:13:39.186 --> 00:14:05.570
<v Casey>That's the honest comparison, and it cuts both ways. Farmers got a pass system because they organized, had political standing, and could pressure a regional district. Caribou, moose, salmon — none of them can lobby anyone for an exception. The obligation to think generations ahead for them falls entirely on whoever's willing to advocate on their behalf, and that advocacy competes for the same depleted provincial attention as the deficit The Tyee wrote about this week.

00:14:06.091 --> 00:14:35.987
<v Riley>Winter ticks on moose are the sharpest version of that same problem. That's a climate signal showing up disguised as a parasite outbreak, and no wildlife branch engineers a colder winter into existence to break the tick cycle. What they can do is track population density and range shift with GPS collar data closely enough to know where moose are moving as habitat quality drops, and adjust hunting allocations or predator management before a population crashes instead of after it already has.

00:14:36.567 --> 00:14:49.559
<v Casey>(deadpan) Which is diagnostic, again, not curative. Knowing exactly how fast a population is declining doesn't slow the decline by itself. What it does is let someone write a very precise obituary for a herd, with excellent supporting graphs.

00:14:50.097 --> 00:15:16.841
<v Riley>It also lets someone intervene earlier than they otherwise would have. The Peace region caribou recovery is the actual counter-example, and it's not hypothetical — GPS telemetry combined with maternal penning took a herd from a few dozen animals up to several times that number over roughly a decade. That's not diagnosis. That's a population that exists today specifically because someone acted on the data instead of just filing it.

00:15:17.432 --> 00:15:33.280
<v Casey>That program also required years of sustained funding, a dedicated penning facility, and active predator control that gets politically contested every single year it continues to run. It worked, once, in one herd, with resources most Cariboo herds have never come close to receiving.

00:15:33.824 --> 00:15:58.672
<v Riley>Which is actually the closest thing to an answer here — not a monitoring tool by itself, but the willingness to fund the unglamorous, multi-year intervention the data points toward, even when it stays politically contested every season it runs. The tool tells you exactly where to put the penning facility. It doesn't build the facility for you, and it doesn't fund the decade of maintenance after that.

00:15:59.355 --> 00:16:17.139
<v Casey>And that's where the province's own budget numbers cut directly against the sentiment. If every year is an austerity budget for environmental protection, as Coste put it, then the conservation programs that require decade-long funding commitments are exactly the ones trimmed first the moment the deficit tightens further.

00:16:17.811 --> 00:16:48.147
<v Riley>There's a Cariboo-specific version of this worth naming directly. The Tŝilhqot'in National Government has, as publicly reported, built habitat and wildlife monitoring authority directly into its own governance structures west of the Fraser, partly because provincial funding cycles couldn't be relied on to persist past an election cycle or a change in minister. That's a structural answer to the funding-instability problem — governance that doesn't reset every time Victoria's priorities shift with a new budget.

00:16:48.798 --> 00:17:03.654
<v Casey>It's a stronger answer than a provincial line item, genuinely. It still requires sustained resourcing on the Nation's own side to run continuous monitoring, and that resourcing question doesn't disappear just because the authority sits closer to the land it's monitoring.

00:17:04.279 --> 00:17:19.439
<v Riley>No, but it does mean the program doesn't vanish the moment a minister changes portfolios or a government changes hands entirely. That's not nothing, especially compared to programs that live or die by a single line in a provincial budget every March.

00:17:20.084 --> 00:17:35.324
<v Casey>(wry) It's not nothing. It's also not the same as knowing, six months from now, whether a specific drainage that burned this August actually gets prioritized for lichen recovery monitoring, or just gets logged as one more hectare in an annual report nobody outside government ever reads.

00:17:35.888 --> 00:17:49.712
<v Riley>Whether the data from this fire season feeds a real decision next spring, or just sits in a database somewhere, is genuinely open. The honest answer is nobody knows yet, including BCWS, because that call hasn't been made public either way.

00:17:50.387 --> 00:18:13.483
<v Casey>Which deserves a concrete ask, not a vague hope that someone's on it. Anyone listening could email the Cariboo Fire Centre or the regional wildlife office directly and ask whether post-fire habitat assessments are scheduled for this year's burn perimeters, and when those results become public. That's a specific, answerable question a regular person can put to a public body without needing any special access.

00:18:14.066 --> 00:18:30.810
<v Riley>It's also fair to ask whether that assessment data gets shared with the Tŝilhqot'in National Government and other Nations running their own parallel monitoring, rather than sitting in two separate systems that never talk to each other, collecting the same information twice.

00:18:31.340 --> 00:18:51.068
<v Casey>That's the structural question this whole season keeps landing on — not whether the technology to track and diagnose these problems exists, because it clearly does. It's whether the institutions holding that data are willing to let anyone outside themselves see it in time to actually act on it, before the next drainage burns and the window closes for good.

00:18:51.609 --> 00:19:09.601
<v Riley>Which might be the more useful thing to carry out of today's conversation than anything about sensor networks or collar telemetry specifically — a technology question quietly turns into a records-access question, and that's a much smaller, much more answerable ask for a community to make of its own government.

00:19:10.113 --> 00:19:21.097
<v Casey>Smaller, and a lot less glamorous than a press release about GPS collars. But probably the one that actually changes what happens to a caribou herd five years from now. (chuckles) Not exactly a bumper sticker.

00:19:21.640 --> 00:19:36.272
<v Riley>If you've got a story tip, a correction, or you want to get involved with any of what we've talked about today, the address is feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a — we read every one, and if you're anywhere near a burn perimeter this weekend, be safe out there.

00:19:36.611 --> 00:19:37.771
<v Casey>Enjoy your weekend, Riley.

00:19:38.099 --> 00:19:41.475
<v Riley>You too — try not to think too hard about the jellyfish.